  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGES:\n\n- Cold front tomorrow with gusty winds and some showers.\n\n- Cutoff low this weekend brings rain and possibly some snow\n  showers to northwest Montana and Glacier National Park. \n\nA cold front moving across the region tomorrow will mainly cause\ngusty winds and some light showers in northwest Montana. A wind\nadvisory has been issued for portions of northwest Montana due to\nthe potential for wind gusts over 40 mph. While predominant winds\nare expected to stay under 30 mph, at least in the lower\nelevations, mountain wave winds are causing the potential for the\nstronger gusts. The usual locations will be the windiest. The\nhighest probability for gusts over 40 mph is along a line from\nHappy's Inn down to Hot Springs with a 70-90 percent chance in\nthat region. There's also about a 40 percent chance in the western\nMission Valley and along the western shores of Flathead Lake. Even\nif the gust 40 mph do not occur along Flathead Lake, the winds\nwill easily exceed the Lake Wind Advisory criteria of gusts over\n30 mph for that area, causing choppy conditions on the lake.\nFinally, the area west of Kalispell near Kila, which is another\ntypical windy spot in northwest Montana has a 70% chance of\ngusting over 40 mph. These chances all come from the 24/12Z run of\nthe HREF, an ensemble of short range, high resolution models. \n\nThe upper level support behind the cold front is a cutoff low that\nwill migrate across the region on Saturday. It brings with it a\nchance for snow in the Glacier Park region. Recent runs have\ntrended down with only a 10-15 percent chance of 1 inch or more of\nsnow at Logan Pass. But don't be fooled by the trends, it will\nstill be a wet, cold, and windy day in the backcountry, so if \nyou're out there be prepared for prime hypothermia conditions. \n\nIn the longer term for mid next week and beyond, the ensembles \nhave an unusual amount of disagreement. Overall, the trend is for \na building ridge, which they all agree well on beyond the 7-day \nforecast. However, from Monday through Thursday, there's \nconsiderable disagreement between the ensembles on how quickly it\nwill warm up. That being said, there's also no real signs of any \nhigh impact weather during that period.",
